  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Current radar and satellite imagery\ndepicts reflectivity over northeastern White Pine and eastern Elko\nCounties this afternoon. Lingering monsoonal moisture over the\neastern NV border combined with sufficient CAPE values (150-300\nJ/kg) is helping keep activity over the region through Monday\nevening. Showers and storms are relatively quick moving from\nsouthwest to northeast so rainfall amounts are forecast to be\nfairly light. Biggest impacts will be new fire starts from\nlightning strikes and gusty erratic winds that will aggravate\nexisting fire behavior.\n\nSpeaking of fires, smoke from ongoing wildfires in Elko County and\nsouthern Idaho will continue to impact visibility in the area and\ncreate respiratory issues for some. Southwest flow today is\nhelping push smoke from ID out of northern NV this afternoon and \nevening. Overnight northerly flow will help smoke filter back \ninto northern NV leading to a smoky Tuesday morning sunrise.\n\nStorm activity on Tuesday will be confined to mainly the UT border\nand extreme northeastern corner of Elko County. Monsoon moisture\nis slowly be shunted eastward as the HPC over the Panhandles\ncontinues to deepen and expand through the week. There exists a\nlow chance of storm activity over the UT border again Wednesday\nafternoon but beyond mid week the availability of moisture really\nbecomes cutoff as the HPC expands westward. A prolonged heat event\nis anticipated with probabilities of temperatures greater than\n100F climbing above 75% for local climate sites Friday through\nSunday. Long-term guidance keeps the HPC and hot, dry conditions\nover the region well into next week.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Moderate confidence exists in\nshower and storm activity over eastern NV today. High confidence\nbuilds in smoke both today and through the week causing visibility\nand respiratory issues as area wildfire activity continues. High\nconfidence exists in the warming trend expected later this week\nthat will bring major HeatRisk to the region by Friday and the\nweekend.",
